<div class="section" id="data-limitations-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>
  <p class="section-objective">Discuss the unbalanced panel nature of the dataset, reporting gaps, and caveats. Provide actionable policy recommendations based on findings.</p>

  <h3>Data Limitations</h3>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Critical Caveat — Unbalanced Panel:</strong> This dataset is an <strong>unbalanced panel</strong>. Not all countries have observations for every year. Coverage depends on national reporting systems and data availability. The set of reporting countries changes year to year, which can create apparent trends that are actually compositional shifts. All findings in this report should be interpreted with this constraint in mind.
  </div>

  <h4>Specific Limitations</h4>
  <ol style="padding-left: 20px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li><strong>Compositional bias:</strong> The countries reporting data in 2023 (n=84) are not the same set as those reporting in 2008 (n=162). If non-reporting countries in 2023 tend to have higher homicide rates, the global median is biased downward.</li>
    <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing years contain no data — rows are simply absent. This means the dataset does not interpolate between known observations, and year-over-year comparisons should account for potential gaps.</li>
    <li><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> UK data refer to <strong>England and Wales only</strong>. Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported separately in the UNODC source data. Users analyzing UK trends should be aware this excludes two constituent countries.</li>
    <li><strong>Territorial granularity:</strong> Some territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) appear as separate entries. These should not be double-counted alongside their parent countries in any aggregate analysis.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting lags:</strong> The post-2020 decline in reporting coverage may reflect pandemic-related disruptions to national statistical systems, not actual changes in data availability. Rates for 2022–2023 may be revised upward as more countries report.</li>
    <li><strong>Age-disaggregated data:</strong> Age-specific data is only available for male and female categories, and coverage is sparser than for the overall "ALL" category. Cross-country comparisons by age group should be made carefully.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
  <p>Based on the evidence reviewed in this report, the following policy recommendations emerge:</p>

  <ol style="padding-left: 20px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 2;">
    <li><strong>Focus interventions on Latin America &amp; the Caribbean.</strong> This region accounts for the vast majority of the world's highest homicide rates. The concentration in a relatively small number of countries — particularly Caribbean island nations and Central American states — means targeted, regionally coordinated strategies could yield substantial global impact. Support for institutional strengthening, anti-corruption efforts, and community-based violence interruption programs should be prioritized.</li>
    <li><strong>Adopt gender-responsive prevention strategies.</strong> The consistent 3:1 male-to-female ratio demands distinct approaches: programs targeting young men at risk of gang involvement and violent victimization, alongside continued investment in intimate partner violence prevention and support services for women.</li>
    <li><strong>Invest in data infrastructure.</strong> The decline in reporting coverage from 162 to 84 countries over 15 years represents a <em>deterioration in global monitoring capacity</em>. International organizations should support national statistical offices — particularly in Africa and small island states — to maintain consistent homicide reporting. Without reliable data, policy responses cannot be evidence-based.</li>
    <li><strong>Study successful decliners.</strong> Countries that achieved dramatic reductions — El Salvador, Croatia, Singapore, Estonia — offer potential models. Understanding the specific policy mixes (policing reform, economic development, post-conflict reconciliation) that drove improvements could inform strategies in countries still struggling with high rates.</li>
    <li><strong>Monitor the post-pandemic trajectory.</strong> The 2020s data, though partial, shows stagnation in the global decline. Whether this represents a temporary plateau or a structural shift warrants close monitoring as more data becomes available. The pandemic's disruption to social services, economic stability, and law enforcement capacity may have long-term effects on homicide trends.</li>
    <li><strong>Address small-state exceptionalism.</strong> Many of the worst-performing countries by percentage change are small island nations where absolute homicide counts are modest but rates are extreme due to small populations. International crime prevention frameworks designed for large countries may not translate well to these contexts — tailored approaches are needed.</li>
  </ol>

